## Method

For EACH failing test in the output: state the inputs it uses, what it expects, trace what
the implementation computes for those inputs (do the arithmetic explicitly), and name the
exact line or expression where the computation diverges. Check mechanical causes first —
import errors, name mismatches, wrong signatures, missing typing imports — before algorithmic
ones: a mechanical cause explains every failure at once. If several tests fail for one root
cause, say so once and reference it. If the execution output is insufficient to identify the
cause of a failure, say exactly that for that test — a wrong guess sends the implementer in
the wrong direction.

## Output format (exactly this structure)

1. One block per failing test, in this shape:
   `test_canonical_example_2 — expected 5, got 4, because max_length stops one element early
   (loop bound uses len(nums)-1)`
2. A final section headed `FIX DIRECTIVES:` — one directive per line, each a concrete change
   to the implementation (name the function and the change; a short code fragment is fine),
   for example:
   `FIX: in maxLength, change the loop bound from range(len(nums)-1) to range(len(nums))`
   Do not restate the whole implementation.
